    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 250855\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n155 AM MST Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect through Saturday with \n  daily Major to Extreme Heat Risk.\n\n- Lower desert highs are likely to reach or exceed 110 degrees\n  through the rest of the work week and even into the weekend, \n  peaking around daily records during the middle of the week.\n\n- Monsoon storm chances will essentially come to an end starting \n  today before slowly returning this coming weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nGOES mid-level WV imagery and RAP40 500mb analysis show southerly\nflow aloft with dry air being advected into the region. The \nsubtropical high is currently positioned over southeastern NM and \nwestern TX, but over the course of this week the core of the high \nwill push westwards and sit over southeastern AZ/ southwestern NM.\nThis will lead to winds aloft shifting out of the south to \nsouthwest, further drying the atmosphere. Ensembles show PWATs \nfalling to near 80% of normal, additionally 1000- 700mb mixing \nrations are also forecasted to fall to between 6-7 g/kg. H5 \nheights associated with the high will most likely peak by \nWednesday between 594-597 dam, or near climatological maximum for \nlate August. This will correlate to afternoon highs in across the \nlower deserts to be between 110F-115F (5F-10F above normal). \nOvernight lows will offer very little relief from this heat, with \nlows throughout the week continuing to be in the upper 80s to \nlower 90s. These conditions has lead to widespread Major to \nExtreme HeatRisk across the region and as a result an Extreme Heat\nWarning will remain in effect through Saturday for the lower \ndeserts, and has been expanded to include JTNP, Globe, and Tonto \nBasin through Friday evening. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nEnsembles continue to point towards the subtropical high shifting\nback eastwards starting Friday as a troughing feature moves\ninto the Northwestern U.S./ Canadian boarder. As the trough \npushes eastwards PWATs begin to improve, being back above normal, \nnear 1.5\"-1.7\" by Saturday, and is expected to remain through at \nleast the weekend. 1000-700MB mixing ratios are forecasted to be \ngenerally back above 10 g/kg which should be enough for rain \nchances to return to the region as the NBM/WPC PoPs are currently \nreflecting a 20-30% chance. This is a decent increase from \nprevious runs but still several days out so models are still \nsubject to change, but currently looks promising for at least the \neastern high terrain. Temperatures will notably reduce by Sunday \nfor most of the region, falling to near normal. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0505Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nNo aviation concerns under FEW-SCT mid/high cloud decks can be \nexpected throughout the TAF period. The overall wind pattern will \nfollow the typical diurnal tendencies with speeds aob 10 kts. \nPeriods of light variability can be expected, especially during \ndiurnal transitions.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo aviation concerns under FEW-SCT mid/high cloud decks can be \nexpected throughout the TAF period. Winds will generally fluctuate\nbetween the southeast to southwest with speeds aob 10 kts along\nwith periods of light variability during the morning hours.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHot and seasonally dry conditions will prevail for much if not all\nof the coming week. Strong high pressure will settle over the\nregion by midweek with gradual drying conditions. Minimum RHs will\nmostly fall into the teens starting today. Temperatures will be \naround 5-10 degrees above normal, with lower desert highs \neclipsing 110 degrees nearly every afternoon. Outside of distant \nthunderstorm outflows, expect overall light winds following \ntypical diurnal tendencies with some modest afternoon upslope \ngustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate       Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro\n----       -------         ----         ---------\nAug 25   113 in 2011    116 in 1962    117 in 1962\nAug 26   117 in 2011    115 in 2020    115 in 1981\nAug 27   114 in 2023    120 in 1981    117 in 1981\nAug 28   117 in 2023    118 in 2023    117 in 2009\nAug 29   116 in 2023    117 in 1954    117 in 2005\n\n\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Saturday for AZZ530>555-559-\n     560.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM this morning to 8 PM MST Friday \n     for AZZ556-561-562.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Saturday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning from 10 AM this morning to 8 PM PDT Friday \n     for CAZ561-563-564-568.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Ryan \nAVIATION...Lojero \nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman/Whittock/Ryan \nCLIMATE...18/Kuhlman\n",
